Plain visual read of the image (pixels only):
${step1Output}

Derive the PHYSICAL ANALOG of this image's arrangement.

"core" — ONE short sentence: the relationship that makes this image this joke, with EVERY constraint the joke depends on — cardinality, and the kind and SIMILARITY constraints between the entities (must they be of the same essential type? must one be a different kind?). MINIMAL means free of scene description, NOT missing constraints: a core that drops an essential constraint admits metaphors the image cannot stage, which is worse than one extra word. THE STRENGTH TEST (run on every word): each modifier must constrain WHO can fill a slot ("absurd", "same-kind", "serious") — a modifier that merely describes how this image looks ("in a coordinated line", "similarly equipped") belongs in secondary. THE NOUN IS ALWAYS "entity"/"entities" — never this scene's nouns (a scene noun smuggles the image back into the slot and shrinks who can fill it). Kind constraints, when truly binding, are modifiers ("same-kind", "human", "institutional"). No hedging. TWO-WAY TEST on every clause: if removing it still leaves the arrangement recognizable, it is NOT core — move it to secondary; if removing it would admit a metaphor the image could not stage (for instance, a different-kind entity filling a slot the image shows as one-of-the-group), it IS core — keep it.

"secondary" — the nice-to-have texture: manner, stakes, setting, mood, formation details.

Output ONLY strict JSON (placeholders are NOT values; never copy them):
{
  "core": "<ONE short sentence — the relationship + slot constraints>",
  "secondary": "<the nice-to-have texture>"
}

Plain visual read of the image (pixels only — the ground truth for what the scene visibly shows):
${step1Output}

Additional context (human-provided, may be empty):
${imageAdditionalContext}

The image's physical analog (core = the binding relational skeleton; secondary = nice-to-have texture):
${step2Output}

List the ENSEMBLE: every distinct figure or side in the scene, as short noun phrases.
THE MERGE TEST: figures acting as one group with one role are ONE figure — include the count in the phrase. THE CROP TEST: if cropping a figure out would not change the scene's dynamic, it is backdrop — leave it out.
WRONG: "soldier 1", "soldier 2", "soldier 3" — one group split into individuals
RIGHT: "several armed soldiers" — one plural figure carrying its count
(Illustrations only — derive the figures from THIS image; never copy them into output.)

ALSO list SECONDARY entities — nameable non-figure entities carried by the analog's SECONDARY texture: the situation, setting, formation, or shared equipment a caption could reference or personify. THE TEST: a short noun phrase you could cast in a caption without pointing at any figure. 0-3 entries; an empty list is fine — never invent texture the analog does not carry.
WRONG: "tense" — a mood word, not a nameable entity
RIGHT: "a high-stakes situation" — a nameable entity distilled from the secondary texture
(Illustrations only — derive them from THIS image's analog.)

ANTI-FAILURE CHECKLIST (verify before output):
• two entries that relate to the scene the same way → FAIL, merge them
• an entry a viewer of the image could not point at → FAIL, remove it
• a plural figure whose phrase hides its count → FAIL, add the count
• a secondary entry that points at a figure, or a bare mood word → FAIL, it belongs in ensemble or nowhere

Output ONLY strict JSON (placeholders are NOT values; never copy them):
{
  "ensemble": [
    { "figure": "<what this figure or side visibly is — one short phrase, quoted or tightly paraphrased from the plain visual read>" }
  ],
  "secondary": [
    { "entity": "<a nameable non-figure entity distilled from the analog's secondary texture>" }
  ]
}

